HOW TO FIND OUT HOW TO USE ANYTHING ON THIS DISK================================================Four places, in the order worth trying.1.  <command> -?    Almost everything here answers -? with a syntax line.  Fastest check.2.  help <topic>            -- the OS-9 HELP utility    Needs HELPDIR pointing at the help library:        setenv HELPDIR /dd/SYS/HELP        <- the OS-9 shell        export HELPDIR=/dd/SYS/HELP       <- bash, which is what                                             SYS/login gives you        help tar    Verified working.  /dd/SYS/HELP holds 12 indexed topics:    bm, dinfo, effo, help, indent, kermit, modules, move, rendsk, sed,    space, tar.    (effo describes the European Forum For OS-9 itself.)    HELP and HELPINDEX are (c) 1989 Lukas Zeller -- the same author as    os9exec.  His conditions require that help.hlp and the helpindex utility    ship alongside help; both are here.  Full text in DOC/help/Read_Me.    To add a topic: write <name>.hlp in the HELP format, then        chd /dd/SYS/HELP; helpindex <name>.hlp    helpindex refuses files that are not in that format -- "No header found".    A .ndx that arrives WITH an archive may be stale: indent shipped one and    `help indent' still said "Help not available" until helpindex rebuilt it.    Every .ndx on this disk has been regenerated and every topic retrieved.3.  /dd/DOC/<name>/         -- per-program documentation    Man pages, READMEs, .doc files, tutorials, example data.  This is where    the bulk of it is: nearly every program has one.  Some programs ship a    .hlp that is NOT in the HELP-utility format (sdb, gshell, fkeys,    chardef, find and the UNAXCESS menus); those are plain text and live    here only.4.  /dd/SRC/<name>/         -- the C source it was built from    Including the original makefile, which is often the only record of what    options a program expects.5.  man <name>            -- read a manual page    Most programs here have a roff manual page.  `man <name>' finds one    through DOC/MANPAGES and formats it, and `man -k <word>' searches the    names.  Underneath, `man' runs nroff, which you can run yourself, but    it needs TMACDIR pointing at the macro directory FIRST --    without it you get        ***nroff: unable to open macro file /tmac.an    which looks like a missing file and is not.  So:        setenv TMACDIR /dd/LIB      (bash: export TMACDIR=/dd/LIB)        nroff -man /dd/DOC/flex/flex.1    /dd/LIB holds tmac.an (the -man package) plus orig.tmac.an and    unix.tmac.an, two variants that came with the nroff source.  They are    NOT Microware's -- tmac.an is byte-identical to the copy inside the    nroff archive, and appears in no Microware SDK.    Output uses overstrike for bold, so pipe it through `more' or `list'    rather than staring at the raw backspaces.WHICH PROGRAM IS WHICH    DOC/STATUS         works / needs cio / broken -- start here    DOC/INDEX          one line saying what every program IS    DOC/ORIGINS        one line saying where every program CAME FROM    DOC/NEEDCIO-INDEX  the cio-requiring set, and where to get cio    DOC/EFFO-INFO      the EFFO authors' own descriptions AND licence                       conditions -- some programs here say 'no military use'    SOURCES.txt        licence, build method, and what was left out and why